course: dictionary
lesson: 6296
title: Mooring: where a boat is tied up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can be a place, a thing or an action. Mooring can be all three, so the words around it give you the clues. Look for the boat, the rope and the water.
explain: «Never play near harbour edges, quays or mooring ropes; the water is deep and cold and ropes can snap tight.» Quays and harbour edges are places where boats tie up, and ropes can snap tight, so mooring ropes must be the ropes that hold a boat in place. The warning also tells you the water is deep and cold.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word. If mooring ropes means ropes that hold a boat in place, the sentence still makes sense. Check your meaning every time, because a word can change its meaning from sentence to sentence.
fact: A mooring is a place where a boat is tied up, often with a rope to a post or buoy.
fact: Moorings are the ropes, chains and anchors that hold a boat in place.
fact: «Mooring» is also the -ing form of «to moor», which means tying a boat up.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the Rat's boat is tied up at its mooring.
fact: Losing your moorings is a figurative way of saying you lose the things that keep you steady.
exercise: «Never play near harbour edges, quays or mooring ropes.» In this sentence, what are mooring ropes? | Ropes used for skipping games | Long ropes for climbing trees | Ropes that help a boat float | *Ropes that hold a boat in place
exercise: In The Wind in the Willows, the Rat's boat is tied up at its mooring. What does mooring mean here? | A type of fish that lives in the river | *A place where the boat is tied up | A small wooden bridge | The muddy bottom of the river
exercise: Someone says, «She has lost her moorings.» What does this mean? | She has got a new boat | She has lost her way on a long walk | She has lost her boat in a storm | *She has lost the things that keep her steady
test: Which word is the -ing form of «to moor»? | *Mooring | Moored | Harbour | Moors
test: A boat is tied up with a rope to a post or buoy. What is that place called? | *Mooring | Bridge | Fishing pond | Lighthouse
exam: What does it mean if someone says a person has lost their moorings? | *They have lost the things that keep them steady | They can no longer swim | They have moved to a new town | They have lost their boat in the harbour
